{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "\"The social network\" is a 2010 american drama film directed by david finch erdman and written by grant heslov, benjamin meadows, and steven zeplekis. the film is loosely based on the true story of the founding of mark zuckerberg's social networking site, facebook, and the subsequent lawsuits and controversies that followed.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a brilliant but socially awkward college student who creates facebook from his dorm room at harvard university. zuckerberg's story is intertwined with those of his friends and co-founders, eduardo saverin (andrew garfield), and thew dupont (jasper tonioli). the film explores the early days of facebook and the struggles of the group as they try to navigate the complex web of relationships and rivalries that develop.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its attention to detail. the cinematography is stunning, capturing the vibrant atmosphere of harvard's campus and the burgeoning tech scene in boston. the costume design is also noteworthy, accurately capturing the style of the time period.\n\njesse eisenberg's performance as mark zuckerberg is particularly noteworthy. he brings a sense of vulnerability and intensity to the role, capturing the complexities of zuckerberg's character. the supporting cast is also excellent, with standout performances from andrew garfield and jason segel as thew dupont and tyler winklevoss.\n\nthe film's script is clever and witty, with a sharp dialogue that captures the banter and camaraderie of the group. the film's score is also noteworthy, with a haunting and atmospheric soundtrack that perfectly complements the film's tone.\n\nhowever, the film has also been criticized for its portrayal of certain characters. some have argued that the film portrays eduardo saverin as a villain, and that it perpetuates negative stereotypes about certain 